===Etymology===
{{wp|[[Rome#Etymology|Rome § Etymology]]}}
{{ety|la|:bor<unc>|und:<alt:*(S)roum(en)ā><ety:af<ine-pro:*sréwmn̥<ety:af<*srew-><*-mn̥>>><ine-pro:*-eh₂>>|ett:𐌓𐌖𐌌𐌀|tree=0}}
{{unc|la}}.

* Roman mythology derived the name from {{m|la|Rōmulus}}, name of the legendary founder and first king. This is almost certainly a {{glossary|folk etymology}}, and the name of the mythical figure is more likely derived from the city name.
* The word may derive from ''*Roumon-'' or ''*Roumen-'', an archaic name for the [[Tiber]] river that would be derived from the {{der|la|ine-pro|-}} root {{m|ine-pro|*srew-||to flow}} (for which compare {{cog|grc|ῥεῦμα||river}} and {{m|grc|Στρῡμών||[[Strymon]], [[Struma]]}}, {{cog|sq|rrymë||{{l|en|stream}}}}, {{cog|sla-pro|*strumy||stream}}, {{cog|lt|stràuma||stream}}, and perhaps also {{m+|la|rūmen}}). If so, the intermediate source may have been an {{der|la|ine}} {{der|la|qfa-sub}}.
* The word may be of {{bor|la|ett|-}} origin, as {{m|ett|𐌓𐌖𐌌𐌀}} was one of the Etruscan {{m|la|gens|gentes}}, from {{m|ett|𐌓𐌖𐌌||teat}}. Given the lack of a secure Indo-European etymology for {{m|la||Rōma}}, this possibility is most appealing.

